Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
now the scottish first minister nicola  sturgeon has promised to set out her  plans for a second referendum on  independence  setting up the future of the union as  one of the major political issues coming  down the track  the labour party could have a critical  role in shaping scotland's future  but it's been a decade of decline for  the party north of the border  back in 2010 they were the dominant  force in scotland winning 41 of the 59  seats but  fast forward to last december and they'd  crumbled winning just one seat in a  nation  now dominated by the snp in hopes for  improvement of slim  this poll from last month shows them in  third place on just 14  behind the conservatives and miles  behind nicholas sturgeon's party  because of that pressure is growing on  the scottish labour leader richard  leonard four msps and a handful of peers  have now openly called for him to resign  but he's vowed to fight on and he joined  us now from paisley thank you very much  for being with us  so some of your colleagues as we  mentioned are calling for you to resign  are you going to  uh no i'm not um and i think those  people that have been calling this week  for me to step down  have underestimated both my resolve but  also the mandate that i got from the  scottish labor party  look sophie um prior to my election as  the leader just under three years ago  we'd had five leaders of the scottish  labour party in six years  and so the mandate that i was given by  the members when they  convincingly elected me to be the leader  was to campaign on a radical agenda  but it was also to be the leader of the  scottish labor party going into the may  2021 scottish parliament elections  at the same time though surely you can  understand why people are concerned  labour's gone from being the dominant  party in scotland to  almost an irrelevance you won just one  westminster sea in december  you're third in the polls you can see  why people are worried  yeah and this has been part of a  long-term trend but look  the situation that we now face  economically and health-wise because of  the pandemic  has really changed the dial of politics  and i think  you know the people i'm speaking to are  having sleepless nights because they  don't know  when the job retention scheme comes to  an end at the end of october  whether it december they'll have a job  or not  so we are expecting a huge spike in  unemployment  we know that there will be enormous  pressures on the national health service  for example we know that there has been  a crisis  in social care all of these things i  think are moving people's focus on from  some of these constitutional questions  uh brexit dominated last december's  election not only  in the rest of the uk but in scotland as  well i think that there is  um a new climber of politics in scotland  which will allow us to start to get our  messages across  about the need for urgent action to  tackle the job crisis  urgent action to make sure we've got  investment in public services  like the health service and and i'm sure  that um  when we get to the elections next may  people will understand  what we which is that the soulful  of the next scottish parliament and the  next scottish government  has got to be on tackling these big  questions climate change so if that's  all if that's all the case then if  that's all the case then why are people  calling for you to go are they just a  bunch of dis  effective blair rights well look there  are some people who  uh really never accepted the result when  i was elected  back um there are um  you know i i think there are some people  who misread uh  the mood of the party membership um  i was given a strong mandate when i was  elected and i've had a huge  number of messages of support from right  across the labor party and  you know people who would consider  themselves to be on the centre or the  right of the labour party and  i would consider myself to be here on  the left of the labour party  so right across the board uh there has  been a a a massive  um i think strong message of support for  me and for my leadership  and you know at a very point when the  country's in a crisis  eight months out from an election uh the  last thing that people want to see is  the labour party turning itself  in on in on itself and and look others  might  say that others might say with respect  though that the last thing the labour  party needs is a scottish  leader who is failing to get more than  14  in the polls and is only winning one  westminster sea in december  well when i inherited the party it was  in third place  it still is in third place but we've got  a you know we've got an opportunity here  to start to make real progress and rest  assured that i will be campaigning for  every  seat as we go into the um elections next  year  i will be touring the country over the  next few months speaking to people  about the jobs crisis about what we need  to do to uh  to solve the unemployment spike that we  are likely to face  and that's about job retention it's  about job guarantees it's about job  creation  you're obviously keen to talk about  you're obviously keen to talk about  jobs and unemployment in scotland but of  course another big feature of how people  vote  is the question of independence so i  just want to really clarify your view  are you completely opposed to the idea  of independence  even if the snp win a majority in the  scottish parliament elections next year  which many people would see as a mandate  for another  referendum well two things sophie first  of all  i do not believe it serves the best  interests of the people of scotland to  set up a separate scottish state i think  you know the valleys of cooperation  solidarity working together  are ones that drive my politics but  secondly  we've said that going into the elections  next year because of the scale of the  crisis economic  uh the crisis in public services uh the  need for investment  the the priority for the next scottish  parliament and the next scottish  government  should not be around constitutional  questions it needs to be about solving  those social and economic questions  and that's why i've said that we will go  into those elections next year  opposed to a second independence  referendum of course we want reform and  modernization of the british state it is  too centralized and with brexit there  are challenges there about how we can  get  more co-determination between a  government a welsh government  a northern irish executive and the uk  government there needs to be a  greater parity of esteem between those  parts of the uk and  and greater decentralization so just  just be crystal clear i think that the  way forward is for the creation of a  separate scottish state because i do not  just to be crystal clear then even if  the snp win majority  uh in the elections will you be opposed  to a second referendum  on independence in all circumstances  well sophie you'll forgive me but eight  months out from an election i'm not  going to concede  uh the result to the snp you know there  is a there is a there will be a  reckoning  for the smp's record both in the  handling of covid  and over the last it said that education  was going to be its top priority and  actually  um attainment in education the gap  between the poorest  areas and the better off areas that gap  is widening so  so on their record uh whether it's an  education the delivery of public  services on jobs uh on green jobs  uh whether it's their record in their  handling of the pandemic and the  and the scandal of what's happened in  scotland's residential care homes  these are big questions which the smp  will be will be called to account for  and i think when that happens people  will understand  that there are alternatives that there  were alternatives and in the future  there is an alternative way that  scotland could be governed  okay thank you very much for being on  the program this morning richard  leonard there the leader of the labour  party in scotland

